C = [
("h1", "15. The Grounds Side by Side"),
("p", "Claim: When the passages are set in one table, no two give the same limit to the same person on the same ground. The table is the argument of the volume in one view, and the reader is asked to test every row against the verses."),
("sub", "15.1 The table"),
("ctbl", "The ten passages by limit, person and ground.", [
    ("Passage", "The limit", "Owed to", "The ground Paul states"),
    ("Romans 14:1–12", "Receive the weak; the eater not despise; the abstainer not judge", "The brother whom God has received", "God hath received him; he is another’s servant; Christ is Lord of the dead and living"),
    ("Romans 14:13–23", "Put no stumblingblock; destroy not with thy meat", "The weak brother", "Christ died for him; the kingdom of God is not meat and drink; the work of God"),
    ("Romans 15:1–7", "Bear the infirmities; please the neighbour to edification", "The weak and the neighbour", "Christ pleased not himself; Christ received us to the glory of God"),
    ("1 Corinthians 8", "Let not thy liberty become a stumblingblock", "The weak brother", "Charity edifieth; Christ died for him; to wound his conscience is to sin against Christ"),
    ("1 Corinthians 9", "A right laid down", "Those to whom Paul preaches", "That he hinder not the gospel; that he may gain the more"),
    ("1 Corinthians 10:1–22", "Flee from idolatry; no fellowship with devils", "The Lord", "The sacrifice is to devils; the Lord’s table and the table of devils exclude each other"),
    ("1 Corinthians 10:23–33", "Eat the market and the feast; eat not when told it was offered", "The other man’s conscience; Jews, Gentiles, the church", "The earth is the Lord’s; the neighbour’s wealth; the glory of God"),
    ("Galatians 5:1–15", "Stand fast; use not liberty for an occasion to the flesh", "Christ, and one another", "Christ hath made us free; faith worketh by love"),
    ("Galatians 5:16–26", "Walk in the Spirit; the works of the flesh named with a warning", "The Spirit, and one another", "The flesh and the Spirit are contrary; they that are Christ’s have crucified the flesh"),
    ("Colossians 2:8–23", "Let no man judge you in meat, drink or days", "Christ the Head", "Ye are complete in him; a shadow, and the body is of Christ; the commandments of men"),
  ], [0.17, 0.28, 0.21, 0.34]),
("sub", "15.2 What the table shows"),
("p", "The first thing the table shows is how many persons the limits are owed to. A brother whom God has received (Romans 14:1–12). A weak brother for whom Christ died (Romans 14:13–23; 1 Corinthians 8). Those to whom Paul preaches (1 Corinthians 9). The Lord himself (1 Corinthians 10:1–22). The other man’s conscience and the three groups (1 Corinthians 10:23–33). Christ and one another (Galatians 5). Christ the Head (Colossians 2). A reader who treats the limits as one rule, //do not offend the weak,// has taken the second and fourth rows and dropped the rest."),
("p", "The second thing is how the grounds differ. In three rows the ground is what God or Christ has done for the person: God has received him, Christ died for him, Christ received us. In one row it is the Lord’s own jealousy. In one it is the earth’s ownership. In one it is the gospel’s progress. In two it is what Christ has done for the believers themselves: made them free, made them complete. These are not interchangeable. A limit that rests on the Lord’s jealousy cannot be set aside on the ground that no brother is present, and a limit that rests on a brother’s conscience cannot be pressed where no brother is."),
("p", "The third thing is where the grounds meet. Edification appears in Romans 14:19, 15:2, 1 Corinthians 8:1 and 10:23. Love appears in Romans 14:15, 1 Corinthians 8:1 and Galatians 5:13, 14. Christ’s own example appears in Romans 15:3 and, in another form, in 1 Corinthians 9. The passages are not unrelated. They share a manner. What they do not share is a single sentence from which each could be derived."),
("sub", "15.3 The test"),
("p", "The reader can test the table by asking of any limit what would be lost if it were stated in the words of another row. If the limit of 1 Corinthians 10:21, the table of devils, were stated in the words of Romans 14, //do not grieve thy brother,// it would no longer forbid a believer to eat at an idol’s table when no brother was looking. If the limit of Romans 14:15 were stated in the words of 1 Corinthians 10:21, it would forbid the strong man to eat meat in the weak man’s presence on the ground that meat is the devils’, which Paul does not say in 14:14. Each limit says what it says."),
("sub", "15.4 What the volume takes from the table"),
("bul", [
    "No two rows give the same limit to the same person on the same ground.",
    "The limits are owed to many different persons or parties, not to one.",
    "Edification and love are shared manners, not a shared rule.",
    "A limit that rests on one ground cannot be restated on another without changing what it says.",
  ]),
]
